{"question": "A 13-month-old boy is brought to the physician for a well-child examination. Physical examination shows hepatosplenomegaly. A venous blood sample obtained for routine screening tests is milky. After refrigeration, a creamy supernatant layer appears on top of the sample. Genetic analysis shows a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) on chromosome 19. This patient is at greatest risk for developing which of the following complications?", "options": {"A": "Acute pancreatitis", "B": "Myocardial infarction", "C": "Corneal arci", "D": "Cholesterol embolization syndrome", "E": "Cerebrovascular accident"}, "pred_answer": "A", "gold_answer": "A", "question_domains": ["Pediatrics", "Genetics", "Metabolism", "Gastroenterology", "Cardiology"], "option_domains": ["Gastroenterology", "Cardiology"], "question_analyses": {"Pediatrics": "The scenario described indicates a 13-month-old boy presenting with hepatosplenomegaly (enlarged liver and spleen) and a milky appearance of his venous blood sample, which becomes more pronounced with refrigeration due to the formation of a creamy supernatant layer. These findings are indicative of a condition known as chylomicronemia syndrome, also referred to as familial chylomicronemia syndrome (FCS) or type I hyperlipidemia. The genetic analysis revealing a mutation in the apolipoprotein C-II gene (APOC2) further confirms this diagnosis, as mutations in APOC2 are a known cause of FCS. ### Interpretation of the Condition:\n- **Chylomicronemia Syndrome (FCS):** This is a rare genetic disorder characterized by the accumulation of chylomicrons in the blood. Chylomicrons are lipoproteins that transport dietary fats from the intestines to other parts of the body. In FCS, the enzyme lipoprotein lipase (LPL) or its cofactor, apolipoprotein C-II, is deficient or dysfunctional, leading to an inability to break down chylomicrons, resulting in their accumulation in the bloodstream. - **Clinical Manifestations:** The patient's hepato